  "body": {
    "id": "lem:complete-normal-tangent-projection",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For any \\(v\\in L_2(P)\\), put \\(v^0=v-E_P[v]\\), \\(r_v(X)=E_P[Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}v^0\\mid X]\\), and\n\\[\nt_v=v^0-r_v(X)'K_P(X)^\\dagger Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}.\n\\]\nAt an interior law covered by Theorem \\(\\ref{thm:complete-normal-canonical-gradient}\\), \\(t_v=\\Pi_{\\mathcal T_P}v^0\\).  In particular, every population coordinate of the complete-normal projected dictionary in Definition \\(\\ref{def:gap-one-step}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal T_P\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "Zper",
      "X",
      "Kper",
      "Tang"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:complete-normal-projection",
      "thm:complete-normal-canonical-gradient",
      "def:gap-one-step"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The argument is the conditional projection calculation in the complete-normal canonical-gradient theorem with \\(v^0\\) in place of \\(\\psi_P^0\\).  Explicitly, if \\(a\\in\\ker K_P(X)\\), then \\(a'Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}=0\\) conditionally almost surely, so \\(a'r_v(X)=0\\).  Hence \\(r_v(X)\\in\\operatorname{range}K_P(X)\\) and \\(K_PK_P^\\dagger r_v=r_v\\).  Therefore, for every measurable \\(c\\) such that \\(c(X)'Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}\\in L_2(P)\\),\n\\[\nE_P\\!\\left[\\{v^0-r_v'K_P^\\dagger Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}\\}\nc(X)'Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}\\right]\n=E_P[c(X)'\\{r_v-K_PK_P^\\dagger r_v\\}]=0.\n\\]\nThe same Pythagorean calculation as in equation \\((4)\\) of that theorem shows that \\(r_v'K_P^\\dagger Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}\\in L_2(P)\\).  Because measurable multiples of \\(Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}\\) have closure \\(\\mathcal T_P^\\perp\\), the subtracted term is \\(\\Pi_{\\mathcal T_P^\\perp}v^0\\), and the residual is \\(\\Pi_{\\mathcal T_P}v^0\\), as claimed.",
    "justification": "The corrected gap sieve needs a proved generic projection result rather than a definition that asserts tangent membership.",
    "gap": "Projecting only against the aggregate residual vector does not generally place a dictionary coordinate in the full tangent space.",
    "consumer": "The complete-normal gap-inference construction uses this lemma for every projected dictionary coordinate.",
